  "name": "LAMOTRIGENE LEVEL",
  "code": "1135",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong data-start=\"43\" data-end=\"81\">Purpose and Clinical Significance:</strong></p>\n<ul data-start=\"82\" data-end=\"558\">\n<li data-start=\"82\" data-end=\"195\">\n<p data-start=\"84\" data-end=\"195\">Measures the concentration of <strong data-start=\"114\" data-end=\"129\">lamotrigine</strong>, an anticonvulsant and mood-stabilizing medication, in the blood.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"196\" data-end=\"396\">\n<p data-start=\"198\" data-end=\"396\">Used primarily to <strong data-start=\"216\" data-end=\"246\">monitor therapeutic levels</strong>, ensure <strong data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"269\">compliance</strong>, and avoid <strong data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"293\">toxicity</strong>, especially in patients being treated for <strong data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"348\">epilepsy</strong>, <strong data-start=\"350\" data-end=\"370\">bipolar disorder</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"375\" data-end=\"395\">neuropathic pain</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"397\" data-end=\"558\">\n<p data-start=\"399\" data-end=\"558\">Helps in dose adjustment, especially when used in combination with drugs that affect lamotrigine metabolism (like valproate or enzyme-inducing antiepileptics).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Lamotrigine Level</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Elevated (&gt;15 mcg/mL)</strong></td>\n<td>May indicate risk of <strong>toxicity</strong>. Symptoms can include dizziness, ataxia, double vision, nausea, vomiting, and in rare cases, severe skin rashes like Stevens-Johnson syndrome.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Therapeutic Range (3&ndash;15 mcg/mL)</strong></td>\n<td>Suggests <strong>optimal drug levels</strong> for seizure or mood disorder control. Dose adjustments may still be needed based on clinical response.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
